    "case_id": "257",
    "case_summary": "A 44-year-old man presented with a 2-year history of headache, dizziness, and imbalance. He had a history of hypertension and cervical disc herniation. One year ago, he underwent transcatheter closure for patent foramen ovale. His father had progressive cognitive decline and imbalance since the age of 50 years and died of cerebral hemorrhage at the age of 65 years.\n\nNeurological examination revealed brisk bilateral tendon reflexes and bilateral Babinski sign.\n\n- Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI):    Image 1 Title: Axial Magnetic Resonance Imaging of the Brain    Image 1 Description: T2-weighted fluid-attenuated inversion recovery showing lacunar infarctions and periventricular white matter hyperintensities (WMH)    Image 2 Title: Susceptibility-Weighted Imaging    Image 2 Description: Showing a lobe-dominant distribution of multiple cerebral microbleeds (CMBs) and relatively fewer CMBs in deep regions, mainly involving bilateral parieto-occipital lobes  - Genetic Analysis:    Revealed a heterozygous mutation (c.754G>A, p.A252T) in the HTRA1 gene",
